# Source for q/ae-cardiomyopathy.js: Cardiomyopathy, Physiology (LV function/diastology), Measurements.
# Values follow ASE/EACVI chamber quantification (2015) and diastolic function (2016) recommendations.
C="Cardiomyopathy"; PH="Physiology"; M="Measurements"
Q=[
# --- Dilated ---
(C,"Typical echo findings in dilated cardiomyopathy include:","A dilated, globally hypokinetic LV with reduced EF and often functional MR",["A small LV cavity with thick walls","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with SAM","Apical ballooning with basal hyperkinesis only"],"The LV becomes more spherical, leaflets are tethered and the LA enlarges. Look for LV thrombus.","F"),
(C,"Common causes of dilated cardiomyopathy include all EXCEPT:","Amyloid infiltration",["Ischemic heart disease","Alcohol","Chemotherapy such as anthracyclines"],"Amyloid causes a restrictive pattern with thick walls. Other DCM causes include viral myocarditis, genetics, peripartum and tachycardia.","F"),
(C,"An increased E-point septal separation (EPSS) on M-mode, such as 15 mm, suggests:","Reduced LV systolic function",["Normal LV function","Mitral stenosis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"Normal EPSS is under about 7 mm. A dilated, poorly contracting LV keeps the opened mitral leaflet far from the septum. AR can also increase EPSS.","F"),
(C,"A 'B-bump' (A-C shoulder) on the mitral valve M-mode indicates:","Raised LV end-diastolic pressure",["Normal filling","Mitral valve prolapse","Aortic stenosis"],"High LV diastolic pressure interrupts normal closure of the mitral valve after atrial contraction.","C"),
(C,"Functional MR in dilated cardiomyopathy is usually:","Central, from annular dilation and leaflet tethering",["Eccentric, from a flail leaflet","Caused by rheumatic fusion","Caused by SAM"],"The leaflets are normal but can't meet because the LV and annulus are enlarged.","F"),
(C,"In dilated cardiomyopathy with very low EF, the sonographer should look carefully at the apex for:","Thrombus",["Vegetation","Myxoma","Lambl's excrescences"],"Slow flow in a poorly contracting apex promotes thrombus. Contrast helps when the apex is poorly seen.","F"),
(C,"Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy is notable because:","LV function often recovers once the heart rate is controlled",["It never improves","It only affects the RV","It causes thick walls"],"Persistent fast rhythms such as AF can weaken the LV; rate or rhythm control may reverse it.","F"),
(C,"Peripartum cardiomyopathy develops:","Toward the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery, with EF usually under 45%",["Only in the first trimester","Only in men","Only after age 70"],"It is a diagnosis of exclusion with LV systolic dysfunction and no other cause. Many patients recover.","F"),
(C,"Chagas cardiomyopathy is classically associated with:","An apical (or inferolateral) LV aneurysm",["Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Thickened tricuspid and pulmonic valves","A bicuspid aortic valve"],"Chagas disease, from Trypanosoma cruzi, is common in Latin America and causes regional wall motion abnormalities and arrhythmias.","C"),
(C,"Chemotherapy cardiotoxicity is best detected early by:","A relative fall in global longitudinal strain of more than about 15% from baseline",["A small pericardial effusion","Measuring the aortic root","M-mode of the aortic valve"],"GLS falls before EF does. Anthracyclines and trastuzumab are the usual culprits.","F"),
(C,"Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy typically shows:","Apical ballooning with hyperkinetic basal segments, often after emotional stress",["Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Thick sparkling myocardium","A two-layered myocardium"],"It mostly affects postmenopausal women. The wall motion abnormality extends beyond one coronary territory and usually recovers within weeks.","F"),
(C,"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy can be complicated by:","Dynamic LVOT obstruction from hyperkinetic basal segments",["Severe mitral stenosis","Aortic coarctation","Ebstein anomaly"],"Check the LVOT with CW Doppler; obstruction changes treatment (avoid inotropes).","C"),
(C,"Myocarditis on echo may show:","Normal findings, or regional or global dysfunction, sometimes with a pericardial effusion",["Always a thick, sparkling LV","Always severe AS","A bicuspid valve"],"Echo findings are variable. Cardiac MRI is more specific for myocardial inflammation.","F"),
# --- HCM ---
(C,"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is diagnosed in adults by a maximal LV wall thickness of at least:","15 mm, not explained by loading conditions",["10 mm","11 mm","25 mm only"],"In relatives of affected patients, 13 mm or more can be enough.","F"),
(C,"Asymmetric septal hypertrophy is defined by a septum to posterior wall ratio of at least:","1.3",["0.5","1.0","3.0"],"Septal thickening is the most common HCM pattern, but any segment can be involved.","F"),
(C,"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) in HCM is:","The mitral leaflet or chordae moving toward the septum in systole",["The aortic valve opening early","Fluttering of the mitral valve in diastole","Posterior movement of the septum"],"SAM narrows the LVOT and pulls the leaflets apart, causing a posteriorly directed MR jet.","F"),
(C,"Obstructive HCM is defined by an LVOT gradient of at least:","30 mmHg at rest or with provocation",["5 mmHg","10 mmHg","100 mmHg only"],"A gradient of 50 mmHg or more is the usual threshold for considering septal reduction therapy in symptomatic patients.","F"),
(C,"Which maneuver increases LVOT obstruction in HCM?","The strain phase of Valsalva",["Squatting","Passive leg raise","Handgrip"],"Lowering preload makes the LV smaller, bringing the septum and mitral leaflet closer. Standing and amyl nitrite also increase obstruction.","F"),
(C,"Which change makes HCM obstruction worse?","Dehydration or vasodilators that lower preload and afterload",["Beta-blockers","Increasing blood volume","Raising afterload with handgrip"],"Anything that shrinks the LV or increases contractility worsens obstruction.","F"),
(C,"On M-mode of the aortic valve in obstructive HCM, a typical finding is:","Mid-systolic partial closure (notching) of the aortic cusps",["Fluttering of the cusps in diastole","An eccentric closure line","Premature mitral closure"],"Flow suddenly falls when obstruction peaks, so the cusps partly close mid-systole.","C"),
(C,"How is an HCM LVOT gradient distinguished from an MR jet on CW Doppler?","The LVOT signal starts later, is dagger-shaped and lower in velocity; MR is holosystolic and higher velocity",["They look identical","The LVOT signal is diastolic","MR is late-peaking and dagger-shaped"],"Contamination by MR overestimates the LVOT gradient. Use color and pulsed Doppler to locate the obstruction.","C"),
(C,"Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy may be missed on echo unless:","The apex is carefully imaged, often with contrast, showing a 'spade-shaped' LV cavity",["The aortic valve is imaged in PSAX","M-mode of the mitral valve is done","Color Doppler of the TV is done"],"It is linked to deep T-wave inversions on ECG. Near-field clutter can hide apical thickening.","F"),
(C,"Features that favor athlete's heart over HCM include:","A large LV cavity, wall thickness under about 13 mm and normal diastolic function",["A small LV cavity and impaired relaxation","SAM with LVOT obstruction","Asymmetric septal thickness of 20 mm"],"Athlete's hearts have balanced chamber enlargement and normal tissue Doppler. Hypertrophy regresses with detraining.","F"),
(C,"Septal reduction therapy for obstructive HCM includes:","Surgical myectomy or alcohol septal ablation",["Mitral balloon valvuloplasty","Aortic valve replacement","Pericardiectomy"],"Echo guides alcohol septal ablation, often with contrast injected into the chosen septal branch.","C"),
(C,"HCM is an important cause of:","Sudden cardiac death in young athletes",["Rheumatic fever","Carcinoid","Coarctation"],"Screening of family members with echo is recommended.","F"),
(C,"Hypertensive heart disease differs from HCM because it usually shows:","Symmetric concentric LV hypertrophy related to blood pressure",["Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with SAM","Apical aneurysm","Two-layered myocardium"],"Hypertensive LVH rarely exceeds 15 mm and is not usually associated with SAM.","F"),
# --- Restrictive / infiltrative ---
(C,"Cardiac amyloidosis classically shows:","Thick LV walls with 'sparkling' myocardium, biatrial enlargement and thickened valves",["A thin, dilated LV","Isolated RV dilation","A normal study"],"Other clues: a thickened interatrial septum, small pericardial effusion and low ECG voltages despite thick walls.","F"),
(C,"The strain pattern in cardiac amyloidosis typically shows:","Apical sparing, with reduced basal and mid longitudinal strain",["Reduced apical strain only","Normal strain everywhere","Reduced strain only in the RV"],"On the bull's-eye plot this is sometimes called the 'cherry on top' pattern.","F"),
(C,"Advanced restrictive cardiomyopathy shows which mitral inflow pattern?","Tall E wave, small A wave (E/A over 2) and short deceleration time",["E/A under 0.8 with long deceleration time","Normal E/A","Absent E wave"],"High LA pressure pushes blood rapidly into a stiff LV, and filling stops abruptly.","F"),
(C,"Which finding best separates constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy?","Normal or high mitral annular e′ in constriction, low e′ in restriction",["Biatrial enlargement in both","Dilated IVC in both","Short deceleration time in both"],"In constriction the myocardium itself relaxes normally; in restriction the muscle is stiff, so e′ falls.","C"),
(C,"Marked respiratory variation in mitral inflow E velocity (more than about 25%) suggests:","Constrictive pericarditis",["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","HCM"],"The rigid pericardium causes ventricular interdependence. Restrictive cardiomyopathy shows little respiratory variation.","F"),
(C,"A 'septal bounce' on echo is a feature of:","Constrictive pericarditis",["Amyloidosis","Apical HCM","Takotsubo"],"Abrupt septal motion in early diastole reflects ventricular interdependence.","F"),
(C,"Annulus reversus in constrictive pericarditis refers to:","Septal e′ being higher than lateral e′",["Lateral e′ higher than septal e′, as normal","Absent e′","Reversed mitral E/A"],"The lateral annulus is tethered by the stiff pericardium. Normally lateral e′ is higher than septal e′.","C"),
(C,"Expiratory diastolic flow reversal in the hepatic veins suggests:","Constrictive pericarditis",["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Normal physiology","Tricuspid stenosis"],"In restriction, hepatic vein reversal is more prominent during inspiration.","C"),
(C,"Cardiac sarcoidosis may show:","Thinning or aneurysm of the basal septum and regional wall motion abnormalities",["Thickened tricuspid leaflets","A bicuspid aortic valve","Isolated LA myxoma"],"Granulomas cause patchy scarring, conduction block and arrhythmias.","C"),
(C,"Loeffler (eosinophilic) endocarditis can cause:","Obliteration of the LV or RV apex by thrombus and fibrosis",["A dilated aortic root","Pulmonic stenosis","SAM"],"It leads to a restrictive filling pattern and atrioventricular valve regurgitation.","C"),
(C,"Fabry disease is a storage disorder that typically causes:","Concentric LV hypertrophy that can mimic HCM",["Isolated RV dilation","Apical ballooning","A two-layered LV wall"],"It is X-linked. Kidney and nerve involvement are clues.","C"),
(C,"Hemochromatosis affects the heart by:","Iron deposition, causing dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy",["Calcifying the aortic valve","Causing carcinoid plaques","Creating a bicuspid valve"],"Early treatment with phlebotomy or chelation can improve heart function.","C"),
# --- Other ---
(C,"LV noncompaction is suggested when:","There is a thick trabeculated layer with deep recesses and a noncompacted to compacted ratio over 2:1 in systole",["The LV wall is uniformly thin","There is a single thick compact layer","The septum bulges into the RV"],"Color Doppler shows flow into the deep recesses. It usually involves the apex and lateral wall.","F"),
(C,"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC) shows:","RV dilation, regional RV akinesia or aneurysms and a dilated RVOT",["Isolated LV hypertrophy","Thickened mitral leaflets","A bicuspid valve"],"The RV myocardium is replaced by fat and fibrous tissue, causing ventricular arrhythmias, often in young people.","F"),
(C,"Alcoholic cardiomyopathy usually presents as:","A dilated cardiomyopathy that may improve with abstinence",["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Apical HCM","Constrictive pericarditis"],"Stopping alcohol can lead to partial or full recovery.","F"),
(C,"Heart failure with preserved EF (HFpEF) is defined by an EF of:","50% or more, with signs of heart failure and diastolic dysfunction",["40% or less","41% to 49% only","Under 30%"],"HFrEF is EF 40% or less; HF with mildly reduced EF is 41% to 49%.","F"),
(C,"In amyloidosis, a small pericardial effusion and thickened interatrial septum are:","Common supporting findings",["Signs of endocarditis","Signs of tamponade","Never seen"],"Amyloid deposits throughout the heart, including the valves and atrial septum.","F"),
# --- LV systolic function / measurements ---
(M,"Fractional shortening is calculated as:","(LVIDd − LVIDs) ÷ LVIDd × 100",["(LVIDs − LVIDd) ÷ LVIDs","LVIDd ÷ LVIDs","SV ÷ EDV"],"Normal is about 25% to 45%. It reflects only the basal segments along the M-mode line.","F"),
(M,"LVIDd is 50 mm and LVIDs is 32 mm. What is the fractional shortening?","36%",["18%","64%","28%"],"(50 − 32) ÷ 50 = 0.36.","F"),
(M,"LV internal dimensions should be measured:","Perpendicular to the LV long axis, at or just below the mitral leaflet tips",["At the apex","Through the papillary muscle tips at an angle","At the aortic annulus"],"Oblique cuts overestimate dimensions. 2D-guided linear measurements are preferred over blind M-mode.","F"),
(M,"Relative wall thickness (RWT) is calculated as:","2 × posterior wall thickness ÷ LVIDd",["Septum ÷ posterior wall","LVIDs ÷ LVIDd","LV mass ÷ BSA"],"RWT over 0.42 indicates concentric geometry.","F"),
(M,"Increased LV mass with RWT over 0.42 is classified as:","Concentric hypertrophy",["Eccentric hypertrophy","Concentric remodeling","Normal geometry"],"Normal mass with RWT over 0.42 is concentric remodeling. High mass with RWT 0.42 or less is eccentric hypertrophy.","F"),
(M,"Increased LV mass with a normal RWT (0.42 or less) is:","Eccentric hypertrophy, typical of volume overload such as severe AR or MR",["Concentric hypertrophy","Concentric remodeling","Normal geometry"],"Volume overload dilates the chamber with proportionate wall thickening.","F"),
(M,"The upper normal LV mass index (linear method) is about:","115 g/m² for men and 95 g/m² for women",["50 g/m² for both","200 g/m² for men","300 g/m² for women"],"Values above these indicate LV hypertrophy.","C"),
(M,"The ASE-recommended 2D method for LV volumes and EF is:","Biplane method of disks (modified Simpson's rule)",["Teichholz formula from M-mode","Single PLAX diameter","Visual estimate only"],"Teichholz assumes a normal LV shape and is not recommended for EF.","F"),
(M,"Contrast for LV opacification is recommended when:","Two or more contiguous segments are not well seen",["The patient has normal image quality","Color Doppler is being used","The patient is in sinus rhythm"],"Contrast improves border definition for EF, wall motion, thrombus and apical HCM.","F"),
(M,"Global longitudinal strain (GLS) in a healthy adult is about:","−18% to −22% (more negative is better)",["+20%","0%","−5%"],"Strain is negative because the myocardium shortens. Values less negative than about −16% are abnormal.","F"),
(M,"Normal LVEF (biplane) in men is about:","52% to 72%",["30% to 40%","40% to 50%","75% to 90%"],"For women the normal range is about 54% to 74%.","F"),
(M,"The 17-segment model divides the LV into:","6 basal, 6 mid, 4 apical segments and the apical cap",["6 basal, 6 mid and 6 apical segments","4 segments in each of 4 levels","Only 6 segments"],"The 17-segment model is used for wall motion and perfusion. The 16-segment model omits the apical cap.","F"),
(M,"Wall motion scoring assigns:","1 normal, 2 hypokinetic, 3 akinetic, 4 dyskinetic (aneurysmal sometimes 5)",["0 for normal, 10 for abnormal","1 akinetic, 2 normal","Only normal or abnormal"],"The wall motion score index is the average score; 1.0 is normal.","F"),
(M,"Upper normal LV end-diastolic diameter in men (2D) is about:","58 mm",["40 mm","70 mm","80 mm"],"For women the upper normal is about 52 mm.","C"),
(M,"Left atrial volume is best indexed to:","Body surface area, with over 34 mL/m² considered enlarged",["Height only","Heart rate","Weight in kg"],"LA volume index reflects chronic exposure to raised filling pressure.","F"),
(M,"TAPSE (tricuspid annular plane systolic excursion) below about 17 mm suggests:","RV systolic dysfunction",["Normal RV function","Tricuspid stenosis","LV dysfunction only"],"Other RV function measures: tissue Doppler s′ under 9.5 cm/s and fractional area change under 35%.","F"),
(M,"RV fractional area change under 35% indicates:","RV systolic dysfunction",["Normal RV function","LV hypertrophy","Pulmonic stenosis"],"FAC = (end-diastolic area − end-systolic area) ÷ end-diastolic area, measured in an RV-focused apical view.","F"),
# --- Diastolic function ---
(PH,"In patients with normal EF, the ASE 2016 algorithm for diastolic dysfunction uses four variables. Which is NOT one of them?","LVOT VTI",["Average E/e′ over 14","Septal e′ under 7 cm/s or lateral e′ under 10 cm/s","TR velocity over 2.8 m/s"],"The fourth variable is LA volume index over 34 mL/m². More than half positive means diastolic dysfunction.","F"),
(PH,"Grade I diastolic dysfunction (impaired relaxation) typically shows:","E/A 0.8 or less, a long deceleration time and normal LA pressure",["E/A over 2","Short deceleration time","High E/e′ over 14"],"Relaxation is slow, so less blood enters early and more depends on atrial contraction.","F"),
(PH,"Grade II (pseudonormal) diastolic dysfunction can be unmasked by:","The Valsalva maneuver, with E/A falling by 50% or more",["Leg raise","Handgrip","Deep inspiration only"],"Raised LA pressure makes inflow look normal. Reducing preload reveals the underlying impaired relaxation.","F"),
(PH,"Grade III (restrictive) diastolic dysfunction shows:","E/A of 2 or more with a short deceleration time and high filling pressure",["E/A under 0.8","Normal E/e′","Long IVRT"],"It carries a poor prognosis, especially if it does not improve with Valsalva.","F"),
(PH,"Mitral annular e′ velocity mainly reflects:","Myocardial relaxation",["Valve area","Stroke volume","Aortic stiffness"],"e′ is relatively independent of preload. Low e′ means impaired relaxation.","F"),
(PH,"Normally, lateral mitral annular e′ is:","Higher than septal e′",["Lower than septal e′","Always zero","Equal to the A wave"],"The lateral wall moves more freely. This is reversed in constrictive pericarditis.","F"),
(PH,"Average E/e′ over 14 suggests:","Raised LV filling pressure",["Normal filling pressure","Severe AS","Low cardiac output only"],"E rises with LA pressure while e′ stays low, so the ratio climbs.","F"),
(PH,"A pulmonary vein Ar duration exceeding mitral A duration by more than 30 ms suggests:","Raised LV end-diastolic pressure",["Normal filling","Mitral stenosis","Low LA pressure"],"When LV pressure is high late in diastole, atrial contraction pushes more blood back into the pulmonary veins.","C"),
(PH,"Color M-mode flow propagation velocity (Vp) below about 50 cm/s suggests:","Impaired LV relaxation",["Normal relaxation","Hyperdynamic LV","Mitral regurgitation"],"Vp measures how quickly early filling spreads toward the apex.","C"),
(PH,"IVRT is typically prolonged in:","Grade I (impaired relaxation)",["Grade III (restrictive)","Severe MR","Constrictive pericarditis with high LA pressure"],"With high LA pressure the mitral valve opens early, shortening IVRT.","F"),
(PH,"For mitral inflow PW Doppler, the sample volume should be placed:","At the mitral leaflet tips in the apical four-chamber view",["At the mitral annulus for E/A","In the LVOT","In the left atrium 2 cm above the valve"],"For tissue Doppler e′, the sample goes on the septal and lateral annulus instead.","F"),
(PH,"In atrial fibrillation, diastolic assessment is limited because:","There is no A wave and beat-to-beat variation is large",["E/e′ cannot be measured at all","The E wave disappears","Tissue Doppler doesn't work"],"Average several beats. E/e′ and DT can still help.","F"),
(PH,"Normal aging affects mitral inflow by:","Reducing E and increasing A, so E/A falls",["Raising E/A above 2","Abolishing the A wave","Shortening IVRT markedly"],"Relaxation slows with age. Age-adjusted normal values are needed.","F"),
(PH,"A TR peak velocity over 2.8 m/s, in the diastolic algorithm, suggests:","Raised pulmonary pressure, consistent with high LA pressure",["Normal right heart","Tricuspid stenosis","Low LA pressure"],"Without lung disease, raised PA pressure usually reflects high left-sided filling pressure.","F"),
(PH,"The phase of diastole that depends most on active, energy-consuming relaxation is:","Isovolumic relaxation and early rapid filling",["Diastasis","Atrial contraction","Isovolumic contraction"],"Ischemia and hypertrophy impair this energy-dependent phase first.","C"),
(PH,"Diastasis is:","The mid-diastolic period of slow filling between the E and A waves",["Atrial contraction","Early rapid filling","Isovolumic contraction"],"Diastasis shortens and disappears as heart rate rises, fusing E and A.","F"),
]
